<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Seaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/2019">2019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Seaga was a Jamaican politician and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica-labour-party">Jamaica Labour Party</a> who served as prime minister from 1980 to 1989. He came to power after an election marked by about 800 political killings, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-manley">Michael Manley</a>'s socialist government. He broke ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and pursued market-oriented policies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-seaga.jpg" alt="Edward Seaga" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A1C Virgil C. Zurbruegg, Andrews Air Force Base · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seaga_at_AFB_Andrews_(cropped).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
